title: "RasterLabel"
version: "1.1"
date-released: "2026-10-12"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Guastella"
    given-names: "Massimiliano"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-9841-6275"
    affiliation: "Department of Civil, Building and Environmental Engineering (DICEA), Sapienza University of Rome, Rome, Italy; Department of Earth and Marine Sciences (DiSTeM), University of Palermo, Palermo, Italy"
license: GPL-2.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/massigit12345/Rasterlabel"
abstract: >-
  RasterLabel is a QGIS plugin, one code base for QGIS 3.36 to 4.x
  (Qt 5 and Qt 6), for annotating rasters with areas, lines and points
  under a legend of the user's own. The work is saved as real geometry in a
  GeoPackage beside a manifest that records the pixel grid, so that a mask
  burned from it lines up with the image pixel for pixel. Readings, an
  operator in a pass, are first class: a second, blind reading of the same
  frames is how the agreement between two readers is measured, with
  confusion matrices, kappa as an agreement statistic, quantity and
  allocation disagreement and frame-bootstrap intervals. A thematic map made
  by any tool can be corrected on a working copy, with a record of every
  change, and compared with the model's map afterwards; the probability a
  model left can send the person first to where it was unsure. Frames can
  be imported with the sampling design they were drawn under. Training
  masks, tiles and STAC catalogues are exported with an ignore value, and a
  dataset card describes what was written. Examples and a tutorial on
  synthetic data are included.
keywords:
  - QGIS
  - raster annotation
  - training data
  - semantic segmentation
  - inter-annotator agreement
  - map correction
  - remote sensing
